a modern-day mona lisa that's how the  chairman of christie's is describing a  portrait of marilyn monroe andy warhol's  shot sage blue maryland is up for  auction one in a series the first to be  auctioned off in nearly 25 years  warhol created the iconic silkscreen  image of monroe after the actress died  of a sleeping pill overdose in 1962.  some 60 years later the public is still  fascinated by her life and death  warhol's genius is because he  encapsulated the tragedy and the  disaster of maryland the suicide after  having the most amazing life being the  most beautiful woman the piece comes  from the private collection of thomas  and doris amman and is the centerpiece  of the auction house's marquee sales  week  warhol's maryland is expected to shatter  records for contemporary art when it  goes up for auction on monday bringing  in the realm of 200 million dollars or  more instantly putting its buyer in an  elite class of collectors  there's no regent that is completely  disinterested it also tells you  something the impending sale is  attracting international interest  why is this piece expected to fetch more  than any other contemporary art piece in  history in this case you get  a beautiful painting by one of the  greatest artists of art history  and you get the most famous painting of  this artist to show the beauty and the  disaster at the same time no other  painting  has ever shown this so well  and perhaps no other painting has been  so well poised to capitalize on  post-pandemic pent-up demand to see and  purchase art  kristin salumi al jazeera new york
 

